import assert from 'node:assert/strict'

export const readingInterpretation=[]
const add=(skill,difficulty,passage,prompt,correct,wrong,explanation)=>readingInterpretation.push({skill,difficulty,passage,prompt,correct,wrong,explanation})
const central=(d,p,c,w,e,prompt='Which choice best states the main idea of the text?')=>add('Central Ideas and Details',d,p,prompt,c,w,e)
const infer=(d,p,c,w,e)=>add('Inferences',d,p,'Which choice is most logically supported by the text?',c,w,e)
const evidence=(d,p,task,c,w,e)=>add('Command of Evidence (Textual)',d,p,task,c,w,e)
const cross=(d,p,task,c,w,e)=>add('Cross-Text Connections',d,p,task,c,w,e)

central('hard','A hypothetical study compares drawings made by beginning and experienced geologists viewing the same rock sample. Beginners reproduce visible color patches; experienced viewers emphasize boundaries that indicate different formation stages. The researchers argue that drawing does not merely transfer an unchanged visual impression to paper. Knowledge helps determine which features become prominent and how they are organized into a representation.','Prior knowledge can shape what observers select and organize when representing the same object.',['Experienced observers necessarily reproduce every visible color more accurately.','Beginners and experienced geologists view physically different rock samples.','Drawing eliminates the influence of interpretation on observation.'],'The same sample yields different emphases associated with expertise. The main claim concerns selection and organization, not a universal accuracy ranking.')
central('medium','In this original story, a fencing instructor teaches a student to notice the instant before an opponent moves. The student first memorizes a list of gestures and reacts too late. During a later practice, she responds to a shift in balance she cannot yet name. The instructor asks her to describe it afterward, allowing action and explanation to develop at different speeds.','A developing practical skill can precede the learner’s ability to explain the cues guiding it.',['The instructor considers explanation unnecessary at every stage.','The student succeeds by reacting to a memorized gesture after it ends.','The story presents memorizing lists as the only route to expertise.'],'The student acts on a cue before naming it, and explanation follows. The instructor neither abandons explanation nor treats memorization as sufficient.')
central('hard','A fictional historian studies the repeated repair of a stone staircase. Replacement steps differ in color and wear, allowing her to identify several periods of maintenance. A proposal to replace the entire staircase would create a more uniform appearance but remove much of this evidence. The historian distinguishes visual consistency from continuity of the physical record, without denying that safety repairs may be necessary.','A more uniform restoration can conflict with preserving evidence of an object’s history.',['The historian argues that every safety repair must be prohibited.','The staircase’s varied appearance proves it has never been maintained.','Replacing all steps would preserve every trace of earlier repairs.'],'The proposed uniformity would erase physical distinctions that record maintenance. The qualification about safety prevents an absolute rejection of repair.')
central('medium','A hypothetical researcher compares two systems for scheduling deliveries. One minimizes travel distance; the other leaves room to accommodate unexpected urgent orders. On ordinary days the first uses less fuel, but on disrupted days the second completes more urgent deliveries on time. The report recommends considering both ordinary efficiency and the kinds of exceptions a service must handle.','The preferred schedule depends partly on whether routine efficiency or flexibility under disruption is the priority.',['The flexible system uses less fuel on every ordinary day.','Minimizing distance automatically guarantees every urgent delivery.','The study shows that disruptions never matter to scheduling.'],'Different conditions favor different designs. The recommendation incorporates both rather than declaring a universally superior schedule.')
central('hard','In this original poem, the speaker compares two measurements.\n\nThe ruler tells how far the shelf\nextends along the painted wall.\nMy hand remembers where to reach\nalthough the numbers leave me cold.\nOne names the distance anyone\ncan check; one knows the cup is there.\n\nThe poem sets publicly repeatable measurement beside familiarity acquired through use.','Different forms of knowing a space can coexist while serving different purposes.',['The speaker proves the ruler gives an incorrect distance.','Familiarity makes every public measurement impossible.','The poem supplies instructions for constructing a shelf.'],'The ruler supplies a checkable distance, while habit guides reaching. The poem contrasts purposes without disproving either kind of knowledge.')
central('medium','A hypothetical team introduces a quieter electric cart into a warehouse. Workers appreciate the reduced noise but initially have difficulty noticing the cart approaching from behind. The team adds a low-volume warning sound that activates near pedestrians. Its report treats quietness and detectability as design requirements that must be coordinated, rather than assuming that the least audible vehicle is always the best one.','Improving one design feature can create a need to address another feature affected by the change.',['The workers prefer maximum noise throughout the warehouse.','The electric cart is louder than the old cart at all times.','Adding a warning means the team has abandoned every benefit of quietness.'],'Reduced noise has both a benefit and a detection consequence. The targeted warning responds to the tradeoff rather than discarding quietness altogether.')
central('hard','A fictional researcher examines instructions attached to a historical measuring device. The instructions specify which marks should be read but assume users already know how to hold the device level. Surviving teaching notes devote many pages to that skill. The researcher argues that a written manual’s brevity can reflect shared practical knowledge rather than a simple underlying task.','Concise instructions may depend on substantial knowledge that their intended users were expected to have.',['The manual explicitly explains every practical skill in detail.','The device requires no skill because its instructions are short.','The teaching notes prove that the measurement marks had no purpose.'],'The manual omits an assumed skill documented elsewhere. That contrast supports distinguishing textual brevity from task simplicity.')
central('medium','In this original story, a choir director records a rehearsal and notices that the sound differs from what she heard among the singers. She moves the recorder to several seats and hears different balances. For the next rehearsal she listens from the audience area before changing the arrangement, recognizing that her working position had given her only one perspective on the combined sound.','The director changes her evaluation after recognizing that position affects the sound being assessed.',['The director discovers that all recordings reproduce identical balances.','The singers changed every note between the recordings.','The story shows that sound cannot be evaluated from any audience seat.'],'Different listening positions reveal different balances. The director responds by testing a position relevant to the performance’s audience.')
central('medium','In a hypothetical materials study, two samples have the same average thickness. One is nearly uniform; the other has a very thin section and a thick ridge. Under pressure, the second sample tears at its thinnest point. The researchers recommend describing local variation as well as the average when evaluating whether a sheet can tolerate stress.','Its local thin section',['Its greater average thickness','Its uniform distribution of material','Its completely different chemical composition'],'The text locates failure at the thin section and gives equal average thicknesses. No chemical difference is reported.','According to the text, which feature of the second sample is most directly associated with its tearing?')
central('medium','A fictional cataloger finds two dates on a shipping record. An inscription on the front records the vessel’s departure, while a note on the back records when the goods were received. Earlier researchers had treated both as departure dates and described a contradiction. The cataloger labels the dates by function before comparing this record with others.','They record different stages of the same shipment.',['They were written in different calendars according to the text.','They prove that the vessel departed twice.','One date has been identified as a deliberate forgery.'],'Departure and receipt are separate events. The text supplies this functional difference rather than calendar variation or falsification.','According to the text, why need the two dates not contradict each other?')
central('medium','In a hypothetical study, participants hear a melody alone and then hear it embedded in a longer composition. Many describe the isolated melody as conclusive but the embedded melody as preparing for something still to come. The pitches and rhythm of the melody are identical in both presentations. The researchers investigate how surrounding musical material affects listeners’ expectations.','The surrounding musical material',['The melody’s pitches','The melody’s rhythm','The number of notes within the melody'],'The melody itself is unchanged; its context differs. This directly answers the comparison without inventing altered notes.','According to the text, which feature differs between the two presentations?')
central('medium','A fictional city survey counts trees along public streets. Its report explicitly excludes trees in private courtyards because surveyors cannot enter them. A later article uses the count as the total number of trees in the city. The survey director requests a correction, explaining that the original inventory’s stated coverage is narrower than the article assumes.','Trees in private courtyards',['Trees along public streets','Trees visible to the surveyors on streets','Every tree included in the public-street inventory'],'The excluded courtyard category makes the original coverage narrower than all city trees. The street trees are the category counted.','According to the text, which category is excluded from the original survey?')
central('medium','In this original story, a young engraver copies a print’s dark background by pressing harder on the tool. Her teacher shows her the closely spaced lines beneath the darkness. She begins varying the distance between strokes while keeping their depth similar, discovering that density as well as pressure can change the visual effect.','The spacing between strokes',['The age of the printing paper','The size of the finished sheet','The tool’s handle color'],'The teacher identifies closely spaced lines, and the engraver varies their distance. The other features are not described as changing.','According to the text, what does the engraver begin varying after the teacher’s demonstration?')
central('medium','A hypothetical study compares notifications sent at fixed intervals with notifications sent when users pause a task. Users receive the same number of messages in both conditions. Those receiving messages during pauses report fewer interruptions. The researchers attribute the difference to when messages arrive rather than to a reduction in the total number of messages.','The number of messages received',['The exact moment every message arrived','The number of reported interruptions','Whether users ever paused their tasks'],'The passage explicitly holds message count equal while changing timing and observing different interruption reports.','According to the text, which feature is the same in both conditions?')
central('medium','A fictional editor preserves a poet’s crossed-out lines in an annotated edition but omits them from the reading edition. She explains that the two books serve different purposes: one allows readers to study revision, while the other presents the final sequence without interruptions. Neither edition claims that the discarded lines were part of the final poem.','To allow readers to study the process of revision',['To claim that every discarded line belongs in the final poem','To eliminate differences between the two editions','To conceal the final sequence from all readers'],'The annotation preserves evidence of revision for a specific use. The passage explicitly distinguishes that use from presenting the final sequence.','According to the text, why does the editor retain crossed-out lines in one edition?')
central('medium','In a hypothetical experiment, a team tests a fastening system under dry conditions and after repeated exposure to moisture. The fastening remains secure during the dry tests but loosens after several wet cycles. The report recommends additional testing for outdoor use and limits its present endorsement to settings where the fastener is kept dry.','Repeated exposure to moisture',['A change in the fastener’s advertised name','The order of letters in the report','A reduction in the number of dry tests'],'The wet-cycle result directly motivates caution outdoors. The other choices do not describe a tested concern.','According to the text, which condition motivates the recommendation for additional outdoor testing?')

infer('hard','In a hypothetical study, an algorithm predicts which archived documents researchers will request. It learns from past requests made through a catalog that described some collections in detail and others only vaguely. The algorithm subsequently prioritizes the well-described collections. The study does not test whether fuller descriptions would increase requests for the other collections.','Its predictions may partly reproduce differences in discoverability rather than only differences in underlying interest.',['The algorithm has measured interest under equally detailed descriptions of every collection.','Researchers have no interest in any vaguely described collection.','Improving a description is guaranteed to reverse every ranking.'],'Catalog detail affects what can be found and requested. The evidence leaves underlying demand and the effect of improving descriptions unresolved.')
infer('medium','In this original story, a seamstress teaches a child to make small stitches by first giving her a loose piece of cloth. Later, she stretches the same cloth tightly in a frame, and the child’s familiar movement produces a different result. Instead of correcting the child’s memory, the seamstress asks her to feel the cloth before beginning again.','The appropriate movement depends on a material condition as well as on remembering a learned action.',['The child has forgotten every step of the earlier lesson.','The frame makes the cloth permanently impossible to sew.','The seamstress believes that touch has no role in sewing.'],'Changed tension alters the action’s result, and the instruction redirects attention to that condition. Memory failure is not established.')
infer('hard','A hypothetical historian finds that two neighborhoods have the same number of recorded shop closures. One neighborhood had twice as many shops at the beginning of the period. The historian has complete closure records but has not yet calculated rates or examined shop types. She cautions against saying that shops in the two neighborhoods were equally likely to close.','The same closure count represents a smaller fraction of the initially larger group of shops.',['The initially larger neighborhood necessarily lost a greater fraction of its shops.','The closure records prove that every shop faced identical risks.','The shop types must be the same in both neighborhoods.'],'Equal numerators with different starting denominators yield different closure proportions. The passage gives no information establishing equal shop composition.')
infer('medium','In this original poem, a speaker describes a bell.\n\nI knew its voice from every field,\nbut standing underneath it now\nI hear the iron shake the air\nand lose the note I used to know.\n\nThe speaker recognizes the bell yet finds its sound unfamiliar at close range.','Familiarity from one listening position need not produce the same experience from another.',['The bell has certainly been replaced by a different object.','The speaker had never heard the bell before.','Moving closer always makes every sound easier to identify.'],'The poem contrasts remembered distant sound with close-range perception. It does not establish replacement or a universal rule about closeness.')
infer('hard','A hypothetical tool reduces the time staff spend entering information, but it requires customers to type that information themselves beforehand. The evaluation records staff time only. A report calls the tool a reduction in total work, although it does not measure customer effort or errors in the submitted forms.','The observed staff-time saving does not alone establish a reduction in work across all participants.',['The tool must increase total work by exactly the staff-time saving.','Customers perform no new task under the revised process.','The evaluation measures customer effort as carefully as staff effort.'],'Work may have shifted rather than disappeared. Without customer measures, neither the overall direction nor an exact offset is established.')
infer('medium','A fictional astronomer keeps a notebook of nights when a faint object is visible through her telescope. Blank entries may indicate clouds, equipment repair, or unsuccessful searches under clear skies. A later reader counts every blank as evidence that the object was too faint to see, although the notebook’s key distinguishes these reasons.','The reader’s count combines missing opportunities to observe with actual unsuccessful observations.',['Every blank records a successful sighting.','Clouds make an object physically cease to exist.','The notebook contains no way to distinguish reasons for blanks.'],'The key separates conditions that prevent observation from negative observations. Treating them alike changes what the count means.')
infer('hard','In a hypothetical experiment, participants practice a task until they reach a fixed accuracy target. One method reaches that target with fewer practice trials. The researchers then test retention a month later but have not yet analyzed those results. A company advertises the method as both faster to learn and longer lasting.','The available result supports faster attainment of the target more directly than the claim about long-term retention.',['The unexamined retention results already prove a lasting advantage.','Reaching the accuracy target requires more trials with the new method.','The method has been shown to be worse on every later test.'],'The immediate trial count is known; delayed retention is not yet analyzed. Lack of evidence for durability is not evidence of worse durability.')
infer('medium','In this original story, a cook recognizes a familiar stew by smell but cannot list all its ingredients. When a trainee asks for the recipe, the cook prepares it slowly and records each addition. She discovers that she adjusts the seasoning after tasting rather than following one fixed quantity every time.','Writing the recipe requires making some habitual judgments explicit.',['The cook cannot prepare the stew successfully.','The seasoning quantity is identical on every occasion.','The trainee already knows every ingredient and adjustment.'],'The cook’s practical recognition and preparation precede an explicit account. Recording reveals adjustments not captured by a fixed list.')
infer('hard','A hypothetical researcher measures traffic only on clear weekdays and finds that a route is reliably quick. The proposed delivery service would also operate on weekends and during storms. The report provides no data for either of those conditions, although it describes the clear-weekday measurements accurately.','The reliability estimate has narrower direct coverage than the service’s intended operating conditions.',['Accurate clear-weekday measurements necessarily describe storms equally well.','Weekend journeys are proven to take longer than weekday journeys.','The report’s clear-weekday observations must be inaccurate.'],'The limitation is untested conditions, not an established adverse effect or false existing measurement.')
infer('medium','A fictional photographer prints the same negative at two sizes. In the small print, viewers first notice a figure near the center. In the large print, they often notice a reflection at the edge before discussing the figure. The image’s arrangement has not changed, but the scale makes some details easier to examine.','A change in presentation scale can influence attention without changing the image’s composition.',['The large print necessarily comes from a different negative.','Every viewer notices exactly the same feature first.','The reflection was physically added to the negative afterward.'],'The same composition produces different attentional patterns at different scales. The passage explicitly rules out a changed arrangement.')
infer('hard','A hypothetical team reports that a repair compound restores a material’s strength. It tests repaired samples only where the original break was clean and aligned. In actual use, damage often includes missing fragments and irregular surfaces. The team has not tested those cases, and its report does not claim to have done so.','A general repair recommendation would require evidence beyond the tested type of break.',['The compound has been shown to fail on every irregular break.','The team secretly tested all missing-fragment cases.','Clean breaks and missing-fragment damage necessarily behave identically.'],'The trial’s scope leaves other damage types uncertain. It supports neither guaranteed success nor universal failure in those cases.')
infer('medium','In this original poem, a speaker finds a glove.\n\nIt fits my hand, though not my days.\nThe cuff still holds another turn:\nthe way she folded it to work,\nthe place her thumb wore through the wool.\nI mend the hole and leave the fold.','The speaker distinguishes making the glove usable from removing every trace of its previous wearer.',['The glove cannot fit the speaker’s hand.','The speaker removes both the hole and the remembered fold.','The previous wearer is identified as a professional glove maker.'],'The mended hole restores function while the retained fold preserves a personal trace. The profession and relationship are not specified.')
infer('hard','A hypothetical survey finds high satisfaction among people who complete an online application. People who abandon the application before the end cannot access the satisfaction form. The report describes the results as satisfaction among completers. A later summary drops that qualification and calls it satisfaction among all applicants.','The later summary extends the result to a group the response process excluded.',['Every person who abandons the application is known to be dissatisfied.','The original report claims that all applicants completed the form.','Completers’ answers cannot describe completers’ reported views.'],'Abandoners are omitted, so the broader claim lacks coverage. Their exact opinions remain unknown, and the original qualified summary can be accurate.')
infer('medium','A fictional historian studies a diary written during a voyage. Some dates were filled in after the traveler returned, as shown by a note in the margin. The events may still be accurately remembered, but the historian separates entries written at the time from those reconstructed later before discussing the diary as a record of immediate impressions.','The diary contains entries with different relationships to the time of the events described.',['Every reconstructed entry is necessarily false.','Every entry was written before the voyage began.','The marginal note proves the voyage never happened.'],'Contemporaneous and retrospective entries differ in timing without that distinction automatically determining truth or falsehood.')
infer('hard','A hypothetical researcher evaluates a model that recommends staffing levels. After managers begin following its advice, waiting times fall. Demand also falls during the same period, and the evaluation has no comparison group facing similar demand without the recommendations. The report records both changes but cannot separate their contributions.','Lower demand remains a possible contributor to the shorter waiting times.',['The recommendations are proven to have no effect.','Demand was randomly assigned to remain constant.','The observed improvement is necessarily due entirely to staffing advice.'],'The simultaneous demand change is an unresolved alternative explanation. The report supports an observed improvement without uniquely attributing it.')
infer('medium','In this original story, a cartographer leaves a blank where a path is said to cross a marsh. Her assistant offers to draw the path from a traveler’s uncertain description. She adds a note instead, marking the report as unconfirmed. Years later, another traveler uses that note to investigate the crossing rather than treating a drawn line as an established route.','The cartographer’s notation preserves a distinction between reported possibility and verified location.',['The cartographer knows the crossing is impossible.','The map presents the path as fully surveyed.','The later traveler ignores the note completely.'],'The explicit uncertainty guides later inquiry. Neither a confirmed path nor a confirmed absence is asserted.')

evidence('hard','A hypothetical researcher proposes that a fish recognizes a shelter from the relation among several landmarks, rather than from one landmark alone. The trained arrangement contains a striped object and two plain objects. She tests configurations in which the striped object and the overall arrangement indicate different search locations.','Which finding, if true, would most directly support the hypothesis?','Fish search at the location specified by the landmark arrangement even when the striped object is moved elsewhere.',['Fish approach the striped object during the original training configuration.','All three objects are visible under the test lighting.','The shelter can be cleaned between trials without changing its shape.'],'Choice under conflicting cues distinguishes the explanations. Training success and visibility do not reveal which cue controls search.')
evidence('medium','A fictional researcher hypothesizes that listeners find a speech easier to follow when pauses mark the boundaries between its major ideas. She compares recordings with identical wording and equal total silence, but with pauses placed either at idea boundaries or in the middle of closely connected phrases.','Which finding, if true, would most directly support the hypothesis?','Listeners identify the speech’s major claims more accurately when pauses occur at idea boundaries.',['Both recordings contain the same speaker’s voice.','Some listeners prefer shorter speeches in general.','The speaker pronounces every word audibly in both recordings.'],'The proposed effect concerns organization of understanding, which the claim-identification comparison directly measures. The controls alone do not demonstrate it.')
evidence('hard','A hypothetical researcher suspects that a surface becomes slippery because a thin liquid film forms under pressure. She compares the same material under conditions that allow the film to form and conditions that remove it rapidly. The surface texture and applied pressure are kept similar.','Which finding, if true, would most directly support the hypothesis?','Rapidly removing the film increases friction, and allowing the film to return lowers friction again.',['The material has the same color in both conditions.','The pressure can be measured with a calibrated instrument.','The surface remains solid during the experiment.'],'Reversible friction changes following the film manipulation support its proposed role. Shared color, measurability, and solidity do not test that role.')
evidence('medium','A fictional researcher proposes that a summary’s examples help readers apply a principle to unfamiliar cases, rather than merely recall its exact wording. Participants receive the same principle with or without examples. The follow-up assessment includes both a quotation-recognition task and a new application problem.','Which finding, if true, would most directly support the proposal?','Example readers solve the unfamiliar application more often even when the groups recognize the principle’s wording equally well.',['Both groups see the principle in the same font.','Some readers say the examples are pleasant to read.','The quotation-recognition task contains four answer choices.'],'A selective application advantage beyond equal wording recognition matches the proposed benefit. Appearance and preference do not establish transfer.')
evidence('hard','A hypothetical researcher claims that a new inventory system reduces missing items by making responsibility for each transfer visible. She distinguishes this mechanism from simply increasing the number of inspections. Two versions have equal inspection schedules, but only one records who hands each item to its next custodian.','Which finding, if true, would most directly support the claim?','The version recording custodians has fewer unexplained losses than the version with equally frequent inspections but no custodian record.',['Both versions list the same categories of equipment.','Inspection schedules are printed in the same color.','Some workers recognize the names of the items.'],'Holding inspection frequency similar while changing transfer responsibility tests the proposed mechanism more directly than the other findings.')
evidence('medium','A fictional researcher hypothesizes that a word’s repetition in different contexts helps learners understand its range of uses. She compares equal numbers of encounters with a word in one repeated sentence or in several distinct sentences. She tests interpretation in a new sentence rather than recognition of the training sentences.','Which finding, if true, would most directly support the hypothesis?','Learners exposed to varied sentences interpret the word in the new sentence more accurately.',['The repeated sentence contains fewer total characters than the varied set.','All learners can recognize the word’s printed shape.','Some learners prefer one training sentence’s topic.'],'A new-context interpretation advantage directly measures flexible understanding. Recognition and topic preferences do not test the proposed transfer.')
evidence('hard','A hypothetical researcher proposes that apparent inactivity in an animal population reflects a shift toward nighttime activity. Earlier surveys occurred only during daylight. She repeats observations with comparable effort during both day and night, using the same locations and an appropriate detection method for each period.','Which finding, if true, would most directly support the proposal?','Reduced daytime observations are offset by increased nighttime observations, with the combined count remaining similar.',['One animal is seen during the original daytime survey.','The survey locations have been assigned consistent names.','The researchers can describe the animals’ usual body color.'],'The compensating temporal shift matches the hypothesis more directly than a simple isolated sighting or survey administration detail.')
evidence('medium','A fictional researcher claims that a diagram helps readers detect an inconsistency between two statements. The statements are unchanged across conditions, but one group receives a diagram showing their relationship. The researcher distinguishes detecting the contradiction from merely recalling both sentences afterward.','Which finding, if true, would most directly support the claim?','Diagram readers identify the contradiction more often despite similar recall of the two statements in both groups.',['The diagram uses straight rather than curved lines.','Both statements contain the same number of words.','Readers can name the title of the document.'],'The comparison isolates the claimed relational benefit beyond equal recall. Visual style and title knowledge do not show contradiction detection.')
evidence('hard','A hypothetical researcher hypothesizes that a noise barrier improves concentration entirely by lowering sound volume. The barrier also blocks moving objects from view. She predicts that reducing volume should produce the benefit even when visual distractions remain unchanged. A follow-up independently varies sound reduction and visual screening.','Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the hypothesis?','Visual screening improves concentration without changing volume, while sound reduction alone produces no improvement.',['The barrier can be installed in more than one room.','Participants can hear some sound in both conditions.','The concentration task has a fixed duration.'],'The controlled result favors the visual alternative and finds no sound-only benefit. That directly challenges the exclusive volume explanation.')
evidence('medium','A fictional researcher attributes a rise in workshop attendance solely to a new advertisement. At the same time, the workshop fee was removed. She predicts that the advertisement should raise attendance even when the fee stays unchanged. Organizers compare events with matched fees but different advertising and events with matched advertising but different fees.','Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the explanation?','Attendance changes with fee removal but not with the advertisement when fees are held constant.',['The advertisement names the workshop’s date correctly.','Some events take place in the same building.','Attendees can remember the organizer’s name.'],'Separating the factors reveals a fee effect without the predicted advertising effect. The alternatives do not distinguish the competing causes.')
evidence('hard','A hypothetical researcher argues that a material’s fading is caused by heat alone. The treatment that produced fading also exposed the material to intense light. She predicts the same fading under equally high temperatures in darkness and under illumination. Another team varies light while holding temperature constant.','Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the hypothesis?','Illuminated samples fade while equally warm samples kept dark retain their color.',['All samples begin with the same color.','The laboratory records temperatures throughout testing.','The material can be cut into pieces of equal size.'],'Different fading at equal temperatures contradicts heat alone as a sufficient account. Shared starting conditions and measurement procedures are controls, not disconfirming outcomes.')
evidence('medium','A fictional researcher claims that a new online form reduces how often residents need assistance. The form includes fewer fields but removes the help button. She counts fewer help requests and treats this as proof that users need less help. A follow-up separately tracks unfinished applications and users’ stated difficulties.','Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the claim?','More users abandon the form because they cannot find help, despite the lower recorded help-request count.',['The form can be opened on a computer.','The help-request total is recorded each month.','Some residents complete the form successfully.'],'The count can fall because assistance is less accessible rather than less necessary. Abandonment linked to missing help directly challenges the interpretation.')
evidence('medium','A fictional literary researcher argues that an original story portrays a character’s apparent indifference as a form of concealed care. The character rarely expresses concern aloud. The researcher seeks a quotation showing an action that quietly protects another person’s interests rather than simply asserting that the character has hidden feelings.','Which quotation would most directly support the interpretation?','“He said the rain was no concern of his, then moved her bicycle beneath the only dry part of the roof.”',['“He watched the rain from the doorway and wondered whether it would stop before evening.”','“He told her at breakfast that he disliked wet weather more than cold weather.”','“He could not remember where she had left the bicycle the previous afternoon.”'],'The contrast between professed indifference and a protective action demonstrates concealed care. The other quotations do not connect indifference to helping her.')
evidence('hard','A fictional critic argues that an original novel presents expertise as flexible attention rather than rigid obedience to a rule. The central character is an experienced gardener. The critic wants a passage in which knowledge of a general practice is combined with adjustment to a particular condition, without suggesting that all rules are discarded.','Which quotation would most directly support the interpretation?','“She knew the usual watering day, but felt the damp soil beneath the dry surface and closed the tap.”',['“She watered every bed at noon because the calendar had always marked that hour.”','“She forgot which plants needed water and asked the visitor to decide.”','“She threw away the schedule before looking at either the beds or the weather.”'],'The gardener retains general knowledge but responds to observed soil conditions. The alternatives show rigidity, ignorance, or indiscriminate rejection rather than flexible expertise.')
evidence('medium','A fictional scholar argues that an original poem treats departure as emotionally unfinished even after its practical preparations are complete. She seeks lines that contrast completed tasks with continued attachment. A reference to travel alone would not establish the specific tension between readiness to leave and reluctance to separate.','Which quotation would most directly support the interpretation?','“The bags were shut, the tickets checked; I stood and straightened one last chair.”',['“The train arrived at half past nine and stopped beside the western gate.”','“I searched the drawer to find the ticket that I had not yet packed.”','“The road beyond the station curved between two rows of trees.”'],'Finished preparations contrast with lingering attention to the room. The uncompleted packing distractor lacks the practical readiness specified in the claim.')
evidence('hard','A fictional researcher argues that an original memoir shows how a familiar object can preserve a relationship without remaining useful for its first purpose. She seeks a quotation in which practical obsolescence and continued personal significance coexist, rather than a passage describing an object that is simply repaired and used in the ordinary way.','Which quotation would most directly support the interpretation?','“The key opened no door after the house was rebuilt, but I still carried it on the ring she had given me.”',['“Once the bent key was straightened, I used it every day to unlock the cupboard.”','“I bought a new key because the old one had been lost on the journey.”','“The locksmith sorted the unused blanks into separate drawers.”'],'The obsolete key retains relational value through the gift. The other quotations show ordinary function, replacement, or impersonal storage.')

cross('hard','Text 1\nA fictional researcher treats a high number of notes in a student’s notebook as evidence of careful attention during a lecture. More written material, she argues, indicates more processing of the ideas.\n\nText 2\nAnother researcher observes that some students copy words without evaluating them, while others write less but connect claims in later discussion. She recommends distinguishing the quantity of notes from evidence of understanding.','What assumption in Text 1 does Text 2 most directly challenge?','That note quantity reliably indicates the depth of processing',['That students can write notes during lectures','That some notebooks contain more material than others','That later discussion can concern lecture ideas'],'Text 2 offers a mismatch between quantity and understanding. It does not deny measurable differences in note volume.')
cross('medium','Text 1\nA fictional translator favors preserving an unusual image even if readers must pause to interpret it. She believes the pause can reveal a different way of describing experience.\n\nText 2\nAnother translator prefers a familiar expression when an immediate emotional response matters most. He agrees that an unusual image can be valuable, but argues that the purpose of a particular passage should guide the choice.','On which point would both translators most likely agree?','An unfamiliar image can have value in a translation.',['A familiar expression is always the best choice.','Readers should never need to interpret an image.','Every passage has exactly the same communicative purpose.'],'The second translator explicitly accepts potential value in unusual imagery. The disagreement concerns when that value should take priority.')
cross('hard','Text 1\nA hypothetical planner judges an emergency route by its typical travel time and favors the shortest average journey. She assumes ordinary conditions provide a sufficient basis for choosing it.\n\nText 2\nA second planner notes that the route passes a bridge sometimes closed during floods, precisely when emergency access may be most important. She recommends evaluating availability during the events the route is meant to serve.','How does Text 2 respond to Text 1?','It identifies a condition relevant to the route’s purpose that an ordinary-time average can omit.',['It proves the route is slower on every ordinary trip.','It argues that emergency routes have no practical purpose.','It confirms that the bridge remains open during all floods.'],'Conditional availability can matter more than ordinary speed for emergency use. The second planner does not contest every observed ordinary journey time.')
cross('medium','Text 1\nA fictional critic values an actor’s very quiet delivery because it draws attention to small changes in tone. She evaluates a recording made with a microphone close to the actor.\n\nText 2\nAnother critic, seated far from the stage, finds the words difficult to hear. He acknowledges the performance’s subtlety but argues that its success also depends on whether a live audience can perceive those details.','What best explains the critics’ different evaluations?','They assess the delivery under different conditions of access to its sound.',['They disagree about whether tone can carry detail.','They both hear an identical close-microphone recording.','One critic knows that the actor spoke different lines.'],'The microphone and distant seat provide different audibility. Text 2 accepts subtlety but questions its availability to the audience.')
cross('hard','Text 1\nA hypothetical analyst interprets the absence of a chemical signal as proof that a sample contains none of the chemical. She sees no need for a second test.\n\nText 2\nAnother analyst notes that the instrument responds only above a stated concentration. A negative result may mean either absence or a quantity below that threshold. She recommends a more sensitive test when that distinction matters.','Which statement best describes the relationship between the texts?','Text 2 identifies an alternative explanation for the negative result that Text 1 treats as decisive.',['Text 2 proves the sample contains a large quantity.','Text 2 denies that the instrument can ever detect the chemical.','Text 2 agrees that non-detection always establishes absence.'],'The threshold creates a second possible source of a negative result. The second analyst does not establish actual presence or deny detection above the threshold.')
cross('medium','Text 1\nA fictional craft instructor recommends learning a sequence of movements exactly before attempting variations. A stable starting method, she says, can help beginners notice what changes when they experiment.\n\nText 2\nAnother instructor values experimentation but also gives beginners a repeatable initial sequence. He explains that without a reference method, learners may find it difficult to identify which alteration caused a different result.','On which point would both instructors most likely agree?','A repeatable starting method can make later experimentation more informative.',['Beginners should never vary a learned method.','Every alteration necessarily improves the result.','A reference method prevents learners from noticing differences.'],'Both connect a stable baseline to interpreting changes. Neither rejects variation altogether.')
cross('hard','Text 1\nA fictional historian interprets an ornate official document as evidence that an event was important to every resident of a town. The expense of producing it, she says, reveals universal public interest.\n\nText 2\nAnother historian notes that officials commissioned the document to emphasize their own role. She suggests examining other records before extending the officials’ investment to the views of all residents.','What is the main concern raised in Text 2?','The priorities of those who produced a record may not represent the entire population.',['An ornate document cannot describe a real event.','The officials spent nothing on producing the document.','Other records are necessarily less informative than official ones.'],'Production purpose and sponsorship limit inference to universal opinion. Text 2 questions representativeness rather than the event’s existence.')
cross('medium','Text 1\nA hypothetical researcher prefers a warning that produces the fastest average response. She ranks warning sounds by response time among people who hear them.\n\nText 2\nAnother researcher asks how many people fail to notice each sound at all. A warning with rapid responses among a small subset, he argues, may be less useful than one noticed by nearly everyone, even if their responses are slightly slower.','What additional measure does Text 2 consider important?','The proportion of intended listeners who detect the warning',['Only the response times of the fastest listeners','The number of letters in the warning’s name','The order in which the report’s charts appear'],'Detection coverage is distinct from speed conditional on hearing. That is the additional outcome the second researcher introduces.')
cross('hard','Text 1\nA fictional essayist describes a preserved recipe as a complete record of a cooking tradition. The written ingredients and steps, she argues, contain everything needed to continue it.\n\nText 2\nAnother essayist finds that experienced cooks adjust heat by sound and texture, skills absent from the recipe. She values the document but argues that continuing the practice also requires learning judgments that its original writers assumed.','How would the second essayist most likely characterize the recipe?','A useful record whose explicit instructions may depend on additional practical knowledge',['A document with no possible value for continuing the tradition','A complete account that already explains every sensory judgment','Proof that experienced cooks never follow written steps'],'Text 2 retains the document’s value while identifying what it assumes. The disagreement concerns completeness, not all usefulness.')
cross('medium','Text 1\nA fictional designer tests a tool with beginners and finds that explanatory labels reduce errors. She recommends including the labels.\n\nText 2\nAnother designer tests experienced users and finds that permanent labels occupy space they need for larger diagrams. He recommends making the labels available on demand rather than eliminating them, allowing users to choose according to their needs.','Which principle would both designers most likely accept?','Explanatory labels can be useful to some users.',['Every user must always see the same amount of explanation.','Experienced users make more errors than beginners.','Labels should be unavailable in every version of the tool.'],'Both designs retain access to explanation. They differ in whether it should be permanently visible for their tested users.')
cross('hard','Text 1\nA hypothetical researcher credits a treatment with an improvement because treated participants perform better than their own earlier scores. She does not include an untreated group.\n\nText 2\nAnother researcher notes that repeated exposure to the test can itself raise scores. She proposes a comparison group taking the same tests without the treatment to estimate that contribution.','What is the purpose of Text 2’s proposed comparison?','To help distinguish the treatment’s contribution from improvement associated with repeated testing',['To ensure treated participants never take a second test','To prove in advance that the treatment is ineffective','To replace numerical scores with participants’ names'],'The comparison addresses an alternative cause of the gain. It does not predetermine the treatment result.')
cross('medium','Text 1\nA fictional critic admires a poem for using plain words to describe a complex experience. She argues that familiar vocabulary can support subtle relationships among ideas.\n\nText 2\nAnother critic rejects the assumption that complexity requires rare words. He points to how repeated ordinary phrases change their significance as the poem develops, creating difficulty through relationships rather than obscurity of vocabulary.','On which point would both critics most likely agree?','Familiar vocabulary can be used to create a complex poem.',['Rare vocabulary is required for every subtle idea.','Ordinary phrases always retain exactly the same significance.','Plain words prevent readers from interpreting relationships.'],'Both locate complexity beyond word rarity. The second supplies a particular relational mechanism consistent with the first critic’s view.')
cross('hard','Text 1\nA hypothetical planner uses the number of people passing a public fountain as a measure of how many people benefit from it. She assumes each passerby has access.\n\nText 2\nAnother planner observes that some passersby cannot operate its high lever or position a container beneath it. She recommends counting successful use as well as nearby foot traffic before evaluating the fountain’s reach.','What distinction is central to Text 2?','Being near a facility is different from being able to use it.',['A fountain’s height is identical to its water temperature.','All passersby have the same physical capabilities.','Foot traffic cannot be counted in public places.'],'The design can exclude people already present. Proximity alone does not establish functional access.')
cross('medium','Text 1\nA fictional researcher describes a workshop’s surviving finished objects and concludes that its craftspeople rarely made mistakes. The objects display careful execution.\n\nText 2\nAnother researcher notes that the collection was selected for an exhibition of successful work. She proposes examining discarded pieces and practice materials before drawing conclusions about how often errors occurred during production.','How does Text 2 qualify Text 1’s inference?','A collection selected for success may omit evidence relevant to the frequency of mistakes.',['Careful execution in the surviving objects proves they are forgeries.','Every discarded object necessarily contains an error.','The exhibition includes all objects the workshop ever produced.'],'Selection can preserve polished outcomes while excluding failed attempts. Text 2 challenges representativeness without denying quality in the selected works.')
cross('hard','Text 1\nA fictional translator proposes using the same translated term every time a source word appears, arguing that consistency guarantees fidelity.\n\nText 2\nAnother translator notes that the source word refers to a physical opening in one passage and an opportunity in another. She argues that keeping one target term could obscure this shift, even if it creates an appearance of uniformity.','Which assumption does Text 2 most directly question?','That a consistent target word necessarily preserves differences in a source word’s contextual meanings',['That a source word can appear more than once','That a translation can use a recurring term','That the passages contain any words at all'],'The proposed uniformity can hide a contextual change. Text 2 challenges fidelity guaranteed by consistency alone.')
cross('medium','Text 1\nA hypothetical engineer favors components that fit together tightly, because the test device remains stable at room temperature. She recommends minimizing every gap.\n\nText 2\nAnother engineer notes that the components expand when heated. A small planned gap can prevent them from pressing against and damaging one another during operation. She recommends testing the intended temperature range rather than only the initial fit.','How does Text 2 respond to Text 1’s recommendation?','It identifies an operating condition under which an apparently undesirable gap may be useful.',['It establishes that tight components are never stable at room temperature.','It recommends leaving the largest possible gap in every device.','It denies that temperature can affect materials.'],'Thermal expansion supplies a reason for designed clearance. The response is condition-specific, not a universal demand for maximum gaps.')

assert.equal(readingInterpretation.length,64)
const counts={}
for(const row of readingInterpretation)counts[row.skill]=(counts[row.skill]??0)+1
assert.equal(Object.keys(counts).length,4)
for(const count of Object.values(counts))assert.equal(count,16)
